  ],
  "text": "Your phone contains around sixty elements, from about thirty countries. It looks like one object. It is an assembly of the entire planet. Lithium out of Australia and South America. Cobalt largely out of Congo. Rare earth metals mined in several places, and refined almost entirely in one. That is the part people get wrong. The risk is not the mining. It is the refining. Plenty of countries hold the ore. Very few hold the plants that make it usable. The chips come from Taiwan, made on machines built by one company in the Netherlands. Then assembly in Vietnam or India, and shipping straight back out again. Not one country on Earth can build a modern phone alone. Not one. Which is either remarkable cooperation or terrifying dependence, depending on the week. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
